<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-review-opening> {
  ex:rv-n02 dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:l5-analytical-distributions ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:rv-n02-c ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:rv-n02-h ;
    cd:narration "Distribution models connect measurement variability with probabilities. We first introduce densities, cumulative probabilities and quantiles, then develop the Normal, Lognormal, chi-square, Student and F families. The final examples compare observed data with a stated model."@en .
  ex:rv-n02-c rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:p1-route .
  ex:rv-n02-h rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:rv-n02-title .
}

<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-review-p1-scenes> {
  ex:p1-n12 dct:subject ex:l5-topic-z ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:nn-eta-h ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:p1-n12-v ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:v3-n12-f ;
    cd:narration "Standardization expresses distance from the mean in SD units. Uppercase Z denotes the random standardized result; lowercase z denotes a realised value. With known mean 10 mg/L and SD 0.10 mg/L, the result 10.20 mg/L gives z = 2. Standardization changes scale while preserving distributional shape."@en .
  ex:p1-n12-v rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:v3-normal-coordinate .
  ex:p1-n13 dct:subject ex:l5-topic-z ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:nn-devices-f ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:nn-devices-h ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:p1-n13-v ;
    cd:narration "The same concentration can have different standardized distances under different models. With expectation 10 mg/L, 10.20 mg/L is two SDs above the mean when the known SD is 0.10 mg/L, and one SD above it when the known SD is 0.20 mg/L."@en .
  ex:p1-n13-v rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:p1-normal-devices .
  ex:p1-n18 dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:nn-bands-f ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:nn-bands-h ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:p1-n18-v ;
    cd:narration "Central coverage is the probability between two bounds. Subtracting the lower CDF value from the upper one gives the familiar Normal coverages of about 68%, 95% and 99.7% within one, two and three SDs. These bands describe individual measurement results."@en .
  ex:p1-n18-v rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:p1-normal-bands .
  ex:p1-n33 dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:ix-series-h ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:p1-n33-v ;
    cd:narration "These three six-reading series have the same constructed mean and different spreads. Their sample variances summarize those spreads. Repeating the sampling experiment would produce a distribution of variance estimates, as well as variation in the sample means."@en .
  ex:p1-n33-v rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:p1-series-chart .
}

<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-review-pdf-cdf> {
  ex:pc-n03 dct:references ex:cf-readings ;
    dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:cumulative-distribution-function ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n03-c ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n03-h ;
    cd:narration "Three questions guide the lecture: What is the probability of a result in an interval? How much probability lies below a threshold? Which threshold corresponds to a chosen probability? Density, cumulative distribution and quantiles provide the respective tools."@en .
  ex:pc-n03-c rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-questions .
  ex:pc-n03-h rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-questions-title .
  ex:pc-n07 dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n07-c ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:rv-n07-h ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:rv-n07-v ;
    cd:narration "In ion chromatography, repeated measurements of one homogeneous nitrate sample vary because injection, flow, temperature and electronics affect the result. A process model describes how these contributions combine. The following simulation explores an assumed additive Normal model."@en .
  ex:pc-n07-c rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-process-info .
  ex:pc-n08 dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:probability-density-function ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n08-c ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:rv-n08-h ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:rv-n08-r ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:rv-n08-v ;
    cd:narration "The simulation assumes Normal individual results with expectation 10.00 mg/L and SD 0.10 mg/L. Extending the same sequence from 20 to 50, 100 and 1,000 results reveals the assumed shape more clearly. The method's SD stays fixed."@en .
  ex:pc-n08-c rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-growth-info .
  ex:pc-n09 dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:probability-density-function ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n09-f ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n09-h ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n09-v ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-interval-question-item ;
    cd:narration "The histogram summarizes 1,000 simulated results; the smooth curve represents the model density. Probability over an interval is the area under that curve, obtained by integrating the density. Comparing the shaded area with the observed interval fraction connects population-model probability with a finite sample."@en .
  ex:pc-n09-f rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-area .
  ex:pc-n09-h rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-density-title .
  ex:pc-n09-v rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-density .
  ex:pc-n10 dct:references ex:pc-n09 ;
    dct:references ex:theoretical-quantile ;
    dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:cumulative-distribution-function ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n10-f ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n10-h ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n10-v ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-quantile-answer-item ;
    cd:narration "The CDF gives the density area to the left of a threshold. Where the derivative relation holds, its slope equals the PDF. High density therefore produces a steep CDF.\n\nThe quantile function reverses this question: choosing cumulative probability 0.95 gives the threshold with 95% probability at or below it. These tools apply to both the Normal and bounded flat examples."@en .
  ex:pc-n10-f rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-functions .
  ex:pc-n10-h rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-linked-title .
  ex:pc-n10-v rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-linked .
  ex:pc-n11 dct:references ex:pc-n10 ;
    dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ;
    rdf:type cd:SceneDefinition ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:nn-phi-f ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:nn-phi-h ;
    cd:hasSceneItem ex:pc-n11-v ;
    cd:narration "The standard Normal is one particular distribution model. Its density is written lowercase phi and its CDF uppercase Phi. Under suitable central-limit conditions, many small additive influences can yield an approximately Normal result. The model's centre, spread and symmetric shape summarize that behaviour."@en .
  ex:pc-n11-v rdf:type cd:SceneItem ;
    cd:authoredResource "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ;
    cd:language "en" ;
    cd:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ;
    cd:selectsResource ex:pc-phi-chart .
}

